# /etc/resolvconf.conf — managed by dot (packages/dns). VOID ONLY.
#
# openresolv is not optional on Void: it is a hard dependency of `iwd`, so it is
# always installed and its dhcpcd/iwd hooks are always on disk. Void's stock config
# ships a `name_servers="…"` line, which means any subscriber that ever calls
# `resolvconf -a` REGENERATES /etc/resolv.conf and silently replaces AdGuard with
# whatever is listed here. That is exactly how this box came up after the Void
# reboot: resolv.conf read "# Generated by resolvconf / nameserver 9.9.9.9 …" and
# every query bypassed AdGuard.
#
# The architecture (docs/dns.md) is: ONE authority for resolv.conf on Void, and it
# is the static file this package installs. So resolvconf is not given a competing
# nameserver list — it is pointed at a scratch path instead. It stays functional for
# anything that inspects its database; it can no longer own /etc/resolv.conf.
resolv_conf=/run/resolvconf/resolv.conf
